WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.075
Module ten:

2
00:00:01.075 --> 00:00:02.591
risk and position size.

3
00:00:02.591 --> 00:00:08.061
Why drawdown is more dangerous than it looks, how to size a position from the stop,

4
00:00:08.061 --> 00:00:12.542
what the Kelly criterion is, and why constant leverage eats capital.

5
00:00:13.542 --> 00:00:17.938
Drawdown is how far capital is below its previous peak.

6
00:00:17.938 --> 00:00:20.496
Its main nastiness is asymmetry.

7
00:00:20.496 --> 00:00:23.773
After minus 50 percent you need plus 100.

8
00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:28.968
After minus 83, bitcoin's worst fall, a rise of almost six times.

9
00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.206
After minus 96, of 25 times.

10
00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:34.744
Not only depth matters but also duration:

11
00:00:34.744 --> 00:00:38.521
how many days in a row capital stays below its previous peak.

12
00:00:38.521 --> 00:00:43.102
That is the time you will have to sit through without abandoning the idea.

13
00:00:44.102 --> 00:00:47.152
The professional approach starts with a question:

14
00:00:47.152 --> 00:00:49.766
how much am I ready to lose if I am wrong?

15
00:00:49.766 --> 00:00:52.816
Usually half a percent to two percent of capital.

16
00:00:52.816 --> 00:00:57.360
Position size is the risk in dollars divided by the distance to the stop.

17
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.464
At one percent risk, 20 losses in a row take about 18 percent; at ten percent risk

18
00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:05.078
they leave only 12 percent of the account.

19
00:01:06.078 --> 00:01:07.411
A game with an edge:

20
00:01:07.411 --> 00:01:11.144
half the trades bring 1.2 times the risk, half lose one.

21
00:01:11.144 --> 00:01:12.810
The Kelly criterion says:

22
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.476
risk about eight percent of capital, and growth is fastest on average.

23
00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:23.009
More than that and growth slows, and at around two Kelly capital typically shrinks,

24
00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:25.542
even though every trade is profitable.

25
00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:31.052
The simulator plays out a thousand futures of 200 trades each.

26
00:01:31.052 --> 00:01:34.399
At two and four percent risk there is no ruin.

27
00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:40.437
At Kelly, two percent of the futures went broke; at 15 percent risk, 28; at 25, 74.

28
00:01:40.437 --> 00:01:44.074
And in real life the edge is not known in advance,

29
00:01:44.074 --> 00:01:47.566
so practitioners bet half or a quarter of Kelly.

30
00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:52.236
Diversification works when assets do not move together.

31
00:01:52.236 --> 00:01:56.640
The daily correlation of bitcoin and ether over two years is 0.83,

32
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.577
similar with other coins, and in crashes it is even higher.

33
00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:05.582
Ten coins instead of one protect against one particular coin going to zero,

34
00:02:05.582 --> 00:02:08.318
but hardly protect against a market fall.

35
00:02:09.318 --> 00:02:14.097
It seems that leverage of two on a rising coin gives twice as much.

36
00:02:14.097 --> 00:02:19.089
But with constant leverage every swing takes away part of the capital:

37
00:02:19.089 --> 00:02:22.156
minus 50 and plus 50 percent make minus 25.

38
00:02:22.156 --> 00:02:23.511
Bitcoin since 2020:

39
00:02:23.511 --> 00:02:28.361
without leverage a tenfold rise, with leverage of two a sixfold one,

40
00:02:28.361 --> 00:02:30.287
with a 97 percent drawdown.

41
00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:34.566
Leverage of three and five was wiped out back in March 2020.

42
00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:36.648
The main points.

43
00:02:36.648 --> 00:02:39.015
Losses and recovery are asymmetric.

44
00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:41.518
Bet size matters as much as the edge:

45
00:02:41.518 --> 00:02:46.050
even a profitable game ruins you if the risk fraction is too large.

46
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:51.528
And coins fall together, while constant leverage eats capital even when the price

47
00:02:51.528 --> 00:02:51.934
rises.
